# hardware/sample_capture.jsonl — recorded esp32_rf_probe samples for the
# "without hardware" path: no ESP32 board needed to see the 2.4GHz Live Test
# tab work end-to-end. Lines starting with "#" are skipped by read_probe.py
# and by the /live/demo backend endpoint.
#
# Each of the 6 lines below is engineered so ONLY the RF-24-* cause(s) named
# survive taxonomy/rf_root_causes.yaml's required_evidence predicates for that
# snapshot — verified against data.predicates.required_evidence_met, not
# hand-waved. Together they walk through every 2.4GHz-only root cause an
# ESP32 probe's evidence (no spectrum hardware, no wired-side telemetry) can
# actually ground:
#
#   A  ch1   healthy baseline           -> no cause asserted (data gap / abstain)
#   B  ch6   co-channel congestion      -> RF-24-001 only
#   C  ch1   adjacent-channel overlap   -> RF-24-002 only
#   D  ch11  non-Wi-Fi (BLE) interference -> RF-24-003 only
#   E  ch6   client density saturation  -> RF-24-006 only
#   F  ch6   co-channel + client density -> RF-24-001 AND RF-24-006 (deliberately
#            ambiguous — this is what ranked_alternatives / top-3 evaluation
#            exists for; see CLAUDE.md's evaluation targets)
#
# RF-24-004 (legacy rates) and RF-24-005 (cell overlap) need
# clients.legacy_client_count / radio.tx_power_dbm respectively — fields an
# ESP32 probe cannot populate, so they cannot be demonstrated from this
# hardware's evidence alone; a live sample asserting neither will show them
# in analysis_context.missing_fields / data_gaps instead. That gap is real,
# not a bug: it's the adapter correctly refusing to claim evidence it doesn't
# have (see adapters/esp32.py's module docstring).
